] = new picture;\n  for (i = -8; i <= 8; ++i) {\n    draw(graf[n],(i,-8)--(i,8),gray(0.7));\n    draw(graf[n],(-8,i)--(8,i),gray(0.7));\n  }\n  draw(graf[n],(-8,0)--(8,0),Arrows(6));\n  draw(graf[n],(0,-8)--(0,8),Arrows(6));\n\n  label(graf[n],\"$x$\", (8,0), E);\n  label(graf[n],\"$y$\", (0,8), N);\n}\n\ndraw(graf[1],graph(funca,-2,1),red);\ndraw(graf[2],shift((1,0))*xscale(2)*graph(func,-3,3),red);\ndraw(graf[3],shift((1/2,0))*xscale(1/2)*graph(func,-3,3),red);\ndraw(graf[4],shift((-1,0))*xscale(1/2)*graph(func,-3,3),red);\ndraw(graf[5],shift((-1,0))*xscale(2)*graph(func,-3,3),red);\n\nlabel(graf[1], \"A\", (0,-10));\nlabel(graf[2], \"B\", (0,-10));\nlabel(graf[3], \"C\", (0,-10));\nlabel(graf[4], \"D\", (0,-10));\nlabel(graf[5], \"E\", (0,-10));\n\nadd(graf[1]);\nadd(shift((20,0))*(graf[2]));\nadd(shift((40,0))*(graf[3]));\nadd(shift((10,-20))*(graf[4]));\nadd(shift((30,-20))*(graf[5]));\n[/asy]\n\nEnter the letter of the graph of $y = f(2x + 1).$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"b84643a014ad5cd0a125540eb0c05a5d1eb76f04d22980990a38d175512898dd"},"seconds":34.098063707351685,"seed":333112732458106998,"stop":"eos","text":"To determine which graph corresponds to \\(y = f(2x + 1)\\), we need to understand how transformations affect the graph of a function.\n\n1. **Horizontal Stretch or Compression**: Replacing \\(x\\) with \\(2x\\) in \\(y = f(x)\\) results in a horizontal compression by a factor of \\(2\\). This means that each \\(x\\)-coordinate of the original graph is divided by \\(2\\), and the graph is squeezed towards the \\(y\\)-axis.\n\n2. **Horizontal Shift**: Replacing \\(x\\) with \\(x + 1\\) after the horizontal compression would shift the graph to the left by \\(1\\) unit. However, since the function is \\(f(2x + 1)\\), the effective transformation is to replace \\(x\\) with \\(2(x + 0.5)\\). This means the graph is shifted to the left by \\(0.5\\) units.\n\nLet's break this down step by step:\n\n- Start with \\(y = f(x)\\).\n- Replace \\(x\\) with \\(2x\\) to get \\(y = f(2x)\\). This squeezes the graph horizontally by a factor of \\(2\\).\n- Replace \\(x\\) in \\(2x\\) with \\(x + 0.5\\) to get \\(y = f(2(x + 0.5)) = f(2x + 1)\\). This shifts the graph to the left by \\(0.5\\) units.\n\nNow, let's analyze the provided graphs:\n\n- Graph A: This graph shows a horizontal compression by a factor of \\(2\\) and a shift to the left by \\(0.5\\) units. This matches our transformation.\n- Graph B: This graph shows a horizontal stretch by a factor of \\(2\\) and a shift to the right by \\(1\\) unit. This does not match our transformation.\n- Graph C: This graph shows a horizontal compression by a factor of \\(2\\) and a shift to the right by \\(0.5\\) units. This does not match our transformation.\n- Graph D: This graph shows a horizontal compression by a factor of \\(2\\) and a shift to the left by \\(1\\) unit. This does not match our transformation.\n- Graph E: This graph shows a horizontal stretch by a factor of \\(2\\) and a shift to the left by \\(1\\) unit. This does not match our transformation.\n\nTherefore, the correct graph is Graph A.\n\n\\(\\boxed{A}\\)"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{A}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
